e war and portrayed him as a  collaborator so if you went to Germany  and you were seen as part of a  propaganda spectacle yes there was a  there was an effect on one's career if  one lived quietly in France or the  Netherlands or even Germany itself and  worked quietly and sold ones work  through the art dealers who continued  this area in this area sold to  collectors one could continue after the  war with with very little difficulty  and and the modernist artists for the  most part they propagated myths in the  post-war period their accounts they  furthered the mythology of the of the  alienated modern artists during the  Third Reich so Emil Nova for example you  know he wrote his memoirs after the war  and he devoted about 10 pages to his  career during the Third Reich and  basically he portrayed himself as a  victim someone who had retreated to his  studio in the north of Germany in a  rural area and town called se Buell and  he had painted little watercolors the  unpainted pictures as he called them and  and he said because the Gestapo was  inspecting his painting for oil and he  had been prohibited from making oil  paintings he did these these watercolors  that was a story in fact he was never  prohibited from painting with oils he  did have a ban on his profession he was  not supposed to be exhibiting publicly  but he was technically allowed to create  privately and he actually painted about  two dozen oil paintings during the war  so the story that he didn't paint in  oils is not true it's a lie  and in fact his income during the war  was appreciable in 1940 I have one  document from Reinhard Heydrich the head  of the Gestapo the Reich security made  agency in these hydrogen Emil NOLA is  reportedly making 90,000 Reich's Marxist  here how is that possible the average  worker in Nazi Germany makes 2,500  Reichsmarks a year so nolde was making  hundreds of thousands millions of  dollars by the time so how is this  possible  so the reality was that he was doing  just fine thank you but he portrayed  himself as a victim who had retreated to  the kind of inner immigration and that  was pretty common for these modernist  figures too to create narratives and  mythologies and to to to conceal the  more incriminating material I mean I was  very struck by the design the drawings  of modernist heroes like Walter Gropius  and Ludwig Mies van der Rohe  where they're sketching swastikas into  their designs  because both Gropius and Me's were  submitting entries for nazi competitions  where there was the first Reichsbank or  one of these German work fronts  facilities and they were putting in  there they're drawing little swastikas  and it's I think to me it shows how much  they wanted acceptance how much they  wanted an opportunity to work and those  swastika lines were not published in the  1950s and 60s and it's only now with a  generational change that we're getting  to these materials that raise these  questions here is the book it's Jonathan  Petropolis who is the author a professor  of European history at Claremont McKenna  College artists under Hitler  collaboration and survival and Nazi  Germany it's the third in his trilogy on  art and the Third Reich
 

